---

SECTION PURPOSE

This is the social-proof section.

The visitor has now seen:

* the problem
* the philosophy
* the framework
* the person behind the method

Now they need evidence that real people have found the material useful.

The existing website contains testimonials.

We are going to preserve the authenticity of those testimonials while completely modernizing their presentation.

IMPORTANT:

Do not invent testimonials.

Do not rewrite testimonials in a way that changes their meaning.

Do not invent names.

Do not invent photos.

Do not fabricate ratings.

Do not manufacture statistics.

Use only testimonial content that already exists in the project/source material.

---

SECTION HEADER

Create a restrained editorial introduction.

Eyebrow:

THE EXPERIENCE

Headline direction:

"Different men. Different situations. The same shift in perspective."

Use a stronger existing brand statement if one exists in the source content.

Supporting copy should be short.

The goal is to introduce the testimonials without making exaggerated claims.

---

TESTIMONIAL DESIGN

DO NOT create a traditional 3-column testimonial grid.

Avoid:

[star rating]
"AMAZING!"
"John, USA"

That would immediately make the brand feel like an ecommerce template.

Instead create an editorial testimonial composition.

Think:

magazine quotes / interview excerpts / case-study fragments.

---

DESKTOP COMPOSITION

Create a large featured testimonial.

Example structure:

LEFT:

Large quotation mark or subtle typographic mark.

Large testimonial quote.

RIGHT:

Name / attribution.

Optional verified context if the existing source provides it.

Then beneath/around it, introduce additional testimonials as smaller editorial excerpts.

Possible arrangement:

```
              FEATURED QUOTE

 ───────────────────────────────

 "Actual testimonial text..."

 — NAME

 ───────────────

 "Shorter quote..."      "Shorter quote..."
```

Do not force every testimonial into identical cards.

Allow different quote lengths to create visual rhythm.

---

TESTIMONIALS AS CONTENT

Build testimonials from structured data.

Example:

testimonials = [
{
quote,
name,
context,
featured
}
]

Do not hardcode every testimonial separately.

This will make it easy to swap/reorder testimonials later.

---

DUPLICATION CLEANUP

The existing website contains repeated testimonial content.

DO NOT reproduce duplicate testimonials simply because they appear multiple times in the source page.

Deduplicate the testimonial collection.

Each unique testimonial should appear intentionally once unless there is a very strong design reason to reuse it.

---

PHOTOS

If authentic testimonial photos exist in the project, they may be used.

If no authentic photos exist:

DO NOT add generic stock photos.

Use typography and layout instead.

This is preferable to fake-looking social proof.

---

VISUAL LANGUAGE

Continue the established editorial aesthetic.

Use:

* warm ivory
* charcoal
* burgundy
* restrained taupe
* large serif quotations
* thin rules
* generous whitespace

Use quotation marks as a visual device, but do not make them oversized decorative clutter.

---

NO STAR RATINGS

Unless the existing testimonials genuinely contain ratings/reviews with ratings, do not add star ratings.

Do not manufacture a review platform aesthetic.

This is a personal-experience product, not a restaurant listing.

---

NO CAROUSEL BY DEFAULT

Do not create an auto-rotating testimonial carousel.

Static editorial presentation is preferred.

If there are many testimonials, a restrained horizontal interaction may be used on desktop, but it must remain accessible and usable without JavaScript.

Do not hide all proof behind a slider.

---

MICRO-INTERACTION

Very subtle:

* quote fade/reveal
* slight image transition if photos are used
* underline/link interaction

No:

* cards flying in
* spinning
* counters
* star animations
* auto-rotating quotes

---

TRUST DETAILS

If existing source material contains legitimate contextual information such as:

* profession
* location
* situation
* length of use

it may be displayed subtly.

Do not invent context.

Do not expose private information beyond what the original public testimonial contains.

---

SECTION TRANSITION

End this section with a small editorial statement leading into the product:

"Now see what's inside."

Then a subtle text link:

"Explore The F-Formula →"

Do not introduce pricing here.

Do not use a giant sales CTA yet.

The next section will explain the actual product contents.

---

RESPONSIVE DESIGN

Desktop:

Feature one testimonial prominently.

Use supporting testimonials around it.

Mobile:

Stack testimonials vertically.

Featured testimonial first.

Use clear separators.

Do not put multiple tiny quotes side-by-side on mobile.

Keep quotation text readable.

---

ACCESSIBILITY

Use semantic blockquote elements.

Associate attribution with each quote.

Ensure sufficient contrast.

Do not rely on quotation marks alone to communicate structure.

